{"entities":[{"pmid":"12907924","text":"Methylphenidate","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D008774","start":0,"end":15},{"pmid":"12907924","text":"obsessive-compulsive symptoms","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009771","start":24,"end":53},{"pmid":"12907924","text":"treatment-resistant depression","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D061218","start":97,"end":127},{"pmid":"12907924","text":"Alzheimer's disease","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D000544","start":138,"end":157},{"pmid":"12907924","text":"methylphenidate","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D008774","start":173,"end":188},{"pmid":"12907924","text":"obsessive-compulsive behavior","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009771","start":202,"end":231},{"pmid":"12907924","text":"methylphenidate","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D008774","start":278,"end":293},{"pmid":"12907924","text":"fluvoxamine","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D016666","start":310,"end":321},{"pmid":"12907924","text":"psychiatric","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D001523","start":348,"end":359},{"pmid":"12907924","text":"obsessive-compulsive disorder","etype":"Disease","mesh":"D009771","start":394,"end":423},{"pmid":"12907924","text":"methylphenidate","etype":"Chemical","mesh":"D008774","start":441,"end":456}],"gold_relations":"methylphenidate | CID | obsessive-compulsive disorder","metrics":{"precision":0.25,"recall":1.0,"f1":0.4,"tp":1,"fp":3,"fn":0,"rel_precision":0.25,"rel_recall":1.0,"rel_f1":0.4,"rel_tp":1,"rel_fp":3,"rel_fn":0},"pmid":"12907924","relations":["D008774 | CID | D061218","D008774 | CID | D000544","D016666 | CID | D009771","D008774 | CID | D009771"],"text":"Methylphenidate-induced obsessive-compulsive symptoms in an elderly man. An 82-year-old man with treatment-resistant depression and early Alzheimer's disease was started on methylphenidate. Significant obsessive-compulsive behavior ensued but diminished over several weeks when methylphenidate was replaced by fluvoxamine. The patient had no prior psychiatric history, but he had a sister with obsessive-compulsive disorder. It appears that methylphenidate precipitated the patient's pathological behavior."}